﻿

Full blade, analog, photography style, cinematic in the style of … hey Siri, who did Darjeeling express? Asma Kahn? No… Hey Siri, who did the movie Darjeeling express… Wes Andersib. In the style of Wes Anderson. I don’t know if this connects to my other work… But here’s why I take AI doomsday, seriously, but don’t treat it as the default position:

﻿

For every “it’s bad” answer there are an equal number of “it’s good” answers. But for every “it’s good” or “it’s bad” answer… there is a vast proliferation of “it’s something else” answers. I just think the reality is likely much stranger than we can imagine. Among those possibilities are doomsday situations, but there is a far a greater of number of “big oops but not doomsday”, “big oops plus big win” and “big win plus little oops” situations. 

﻿

To put it differently… The AI doomsday scenario is at a maximum, 1/4 of the possibility space… But more likely much much smaller than that. That’s worth taking seriously. That’s worth taking extremely seriously. But it probably is not the likeliest outcome, and it probably is not even close to the likely outcome. The fact is, we’re not really competing with AI for very much. We have more in common with broccoli. We have more in common with fish and fish are quite possibly intelligent. We have more in common with octopodes, and elephants, and cats and dogs… All of which are undoubtedly, intelligent, some of them, perhaps more intelligent than us. I mean, look at the lives, fish live…. The doomsday, if it happens will probably be an accident, not a competitive win. more likely, there will be many small dooms afternoons, dooms middays, and dooms brunch hour hiccups. We don’t know the probability space. We can have an idea of the possibility space. The only way we have to assess directionality is to assess motivation and need. The biggest competitor is always always always always always the one who needs everything that you need: and that answer is always ourselves.

﻿

Nothing is likely… Not to say impossible… but nothing is *likely* to be as big a competitor to humankind as humankind itself. —Nathan McKnight 

﻿

